      "articleId": "0a9f90321c5054dde4ec",
      "headline": "Jadot Nkurunziza mobilizes youth for environmental clean-up in Burundi",
      "dek": "Jadot Nkurunziza, known as \"Giti,\" organizes young people to remove water hyacinth from Lake Tanganyika and promote cleanliness in Ntahangwa commune.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Jadot Nkurunziza, born in 1994 in Nyakabiga, Bujumbura, has become a local figure for his environmental efforts. He spends time at a plant nursery by the Ntahangwa River and engages residents in the Ntahangwa commune on cleanliness. On Mwezi Gisabo Boulevard, he regularly educates young people about sanitation and planting flowers, according to a resident.",
        "Nkurunziza also focuses on Lake Tanganyika, where water hyacinth sometimes threatens local activities and the ecosystem. He mobilizes other young people to remove these plants, an effort an inhabitant near the lake described as rare in their community. His vigilance extends to street waste, with residents reporting he asks people to pick up and properly dispose of plastic they discard.",
        "Nkurunziza's commitment stems from his childhood, where his grandfather taught him the importance of trees for fruits, respiration, energy, and construction. After studying environmental protection in Uganda, he returned to Burundi to apply his training within his community, viewing trees as essential resources for daily life."
      ],
      "body": "Jadot Nkurunziza, born in 1994 in Nyakabiga, Bujumbura, has become a local figure for his environmental efforts. He spends time at a plant nursery by the Ntahangwa River and engages residents in the Ntahangwa commune on cleanliness. On Mwezi Gisabo Boulevard, he regularly educates young people about sanitation and planting flowers, according to a resident.\n\nNkurunziza also focuses on Lake Tanganyika, where water hyacinth sometimes threatens local activities and the ecosystem. He mobilizes other young people to remove these plants, an effort an inhabitant near the lake described as rare in their community. His vigilance extends to street waste, with residents reporting he asks people to pick up and properly dispose of plastic they discard.\n\nNkurunziza's commitment stems from his childhood, where his grandfather taught him the importance of trees for fruits, respiration, energy, and construction. After studying environmental protection in Uganda, he returned to Burundi to apply his training within his community, viewing trees as essential resources for daily life.",
