      "articleId": "d5ae10f8fe6e404febf2",
      "headline": "Egypt and WFP launch market efficiency index to enhance food security",
      "dek": "Egypt's Ministry of Supply and Internal Trade and the World Food Programme have agreed to a joint action plan until July 2027 to develop a comprehensive market efficiency index.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "The Ministry of Supply and Internal Trade and the World Food Programme (WFP) have launched a joint action plan to develop a comprehensive market efficiency index, shifting state market monitoring from basic observation to proactive data analysis. Minister Sherif Farouk stated the ministry is upgrading its tools to rely on more accurate and integrated data, enabling it to monitor price movements, commodity availability, and regional disparities.",
        "This new index will expand the ministry’s focus beyond mere price tracking to measure commodity availability, ease of access for citizens, regional price variations, and quality levels. This broader scope aims to provide a comprehensive market view, enhancing the ministry’s ability to detect early signs of disruptions and make timely decisions. The agreement was discussed during talks between Farouk and WFP Country Director Rawad Halabi.",
        "The action plan also includes developing an integrated system for citizen complaints, tracking issues from receipt to resolution, and analyzing data to identify recurring problems and improve services for subsidy beneficiaries. Specialised training programmes on data and indicators will be conducted in cooperation with the Central Agency for Public Mobilization and Statistics to evaluate the subsidy system’s contribution to meeting nutritional needs."
      ],
      "body": "The Ministry of Supply and Internal Trade and the World Food Programme (WFP) have launched a joint action plan to develop a comprehensive market efficiency index, shifting state market monitoring from basic observation to proactive data analysis. Minister Sherif Farouk stated the ministry is upgrading its tools to rely on more accurate and integrated data, enabling it to monitor price movements, commodity availability, and regional disparities.\n\nThis new index will expand the ministry’s focus beyond mere price tracking to measure commodity availability, ease of access for citizens, regional price variations, and quality levels. This broader scope aims to provide a comprehensive market view, enhancing the ministry’s ability to detect early signs of disruptions and make timely decisions. The agreement was discussed during talks between Farouk and WFP Country Director Rawad Halabi.\n\nThe action plan also includes developing an integrated system for citizen complaints, tracking issues from receipt to resolution, and analyzing data to identify recurring problems and improve services for subsidy beneficiaries. Specialised training programmes on data and indicators will be conducted in cooperation with the Central Agency for Public Mobilization and Statistics to evaluate the subsidy system’s contribution to meeting nutritional needs.",
