      "headline": "Experts urge East Africa to commercialize bioeconomy research",
      "dek": "Policymakers, scientists, investors, and entrepreneurs at the Africa Bioeconomy Workshop in Nairobi called for the region to move beyond research to build profitable industries.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "East African experts are pushing for the region to convert bioeconomy research into profitable industries, moving past strategies and projects. The call came at the Africa Bioeconomy Workshop in Nairobi, where attendees urged governments to connect agricultural production, research, and innovation with markets to create jobs and retain more value from biological resources.",
        "Niall O’Connor, Centre Director of the Stockholm Environment Institute Africa Centre, stated that the region possesses the necessary resources, research capacity, and entrepreneurial talent for a competitive bio-based economy. However, he noted that value is still lost through the export of raw materials. The 2026 State of the Bioeconomy in Eastern Africa report identifies agricultural residues and organic waste as potential feedstocks for industries producing biofuels, organic fertilizers, and other bio-based products.",
        "Dr. Abdou Tenkouano, Director General of the International Centre of Insect Physiology and Ecology, highlighted agriculture's significant contribution to GDP in many sub-Saharan African countries, ranging from 23 to 35 percent. He emphasized that this sector offers a major opportunity to develop industries around biomass, urging governments to strengthen university-industry partnerships and support innovation in agro-processing and bioprocessing."
      ],
      "body": "East African experts are pushing for the region to convert bioeconomy research into profitable industries, moving past strategies and projects. The call came at the Africa Bioeconomy Workshop in Nairobi, where attendees urged governments to connect agricultural production, research, and innovation with markets to create jobs and retain more value from biological resources.\n\nNiall O’Connor, Centre Director of the Stockholm Environment Institute Africa Centre, stated that the region possesses the necessary resources, research capacity, and entrepreneurial talent for a competitive bio-based economy. However, he noted that value is still lost through the export of raw materials. The 2026 State of the Bioeconomy in Eastern Africa report identifies agricultural residues and organic waste as potential feedstocks for industries producing biofuels, organic fertilizers, and other bio-based products.\n\nDr. Abdou Tenkouano, Director General of the International Centre of Insect Physiology and Ecology, highlighted agriculture's significant contribution to GDP in many sub-Saharan African countries, ranging from 23 to 35 percent. He emphasized that this sector offers a major opportunity to develop industries around biomass, urging governments to strengthen university-industry partnerships and support innovation in agro-processing and bioprocessing.",
