      "articleId": "699369b28bca6ec62be8",
      "headline": "Johannesburg panel urges youth inclusion for city renewal",
      "dek": "A Metro Lives dialogue highlighted that Johannesburg's urban renewal efforts will fail without the active participation of its young people, who face food insecurity and exclusion.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Johannesburg's renewal efforts depend on the meaningful participation of its young people, according to speakers at a Metro Lives dialogue hosted by the DG Murray Trust at Constitution Hill. The discussion on Monday, August twenty-fourth, emphasized treating youth as co-authors of the city's future, not merely as beneficiaries, to address issues like food insecurity, public safety, and exclusion.",
        "Matshidiso Lencoasa, a budget analyst at SECTION27, stated that young people possess ideas and solutions that need to be foregrounded for Johannesburg to work for all. She joined Dr. Tlaleng Mofokeng, Nomzekelo Mohamed, Mark Heywood, and Ayanda Allie in exploring how local governments can build safer, healthier, and more inclusive communities by centering children and young people.",
        "The conversation also touched on the city's failing urban food system, with one high school learner sharing her experience of going to school hungry. Mark Heywood, co-founder of Union Against Hunger, suggested practical steps like identifying municipal land for food cultivation, ensuring water and security, and viewing the city as a food-producing space, citing the demolition of an inner-city farm as a counterproductive action."
      ],
      "body": "Johannesburg's renewal efforts depend on the meaningful participation of its young people, according to speakers at a Metro Lives dialogue hosted by the DG Murray Trust at Constitution Hill. The discussion on Monday, August twenty-fourth, emphasized treating youth as co-authors of the city's future, not merely as beneficiaries, to address issues like food insecurity, public safety, and exclusion.\n\nMatshidiso Lencoasa, a budget analyst at SECTION27, stated that young people possess ideas and solutions that need to be foregrounded for Johannesburg to work for all. She joined Dr. Tlaleng Mofokeng, Nomzekelo Mohamed, Mark Heywood, and Ayanda Allie in exploring how local governments can build safer, healthier, and more inclusive communities by centering children and young people.\n\nThe conversation also touched on the city's failing urban food system, with one high school learner sharing her experience of going to school hungry. Mark Heywood, co-founder of Union Against Hunger, suggested practical steps like identifying municipal land for food cultivation, ensuring water and security, and viewing the city as a food-producing space, citing the demolition of an inner-city farm as a counterproductive action.",
