      "headline": "Tanzania seeks global investment to restore degraded land",
      "dek": "Tanzania has called on the international community to increase investment, technology, and financial resources to combat desertification and land degradation.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Tanzania's Minister of State, Vice President's Office (Union and Environment), Hamad Masauni, made the appeal at the 17th Conference of Parties to the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD COP17) in Ulaanbaatar. The conference, themed “Restore Land, Restore Hope,” emphasizes increasing investment in land restoration, building resilience to drought, and sustainable management of grazing areas.",
        "Masauni highlighted that land degradation in Tanzania stems from deforestation, agricultural expansion, uncontrolled burning, soil erosion, unsustainable land use, and urban sprawl. He noted that Tanzania's participation in the conference offers opportunities to gain experience, technology, partnerships, and financial resources to improve sustainable management of grazing areas, restore degraded land, conserve forests and water sources, protect biodiversity, and enhance farmers' and pastoralists' resilience to environmental challenges.",
        "Thimoth Mande, Tanzania's National Coordinator for the Convention, stated that international cooperation is crucial for Tanzania to address land degradation, biodiversity loss, and climate change comprehensively. He added that Tanzania's involvement aims to attract more resources from environmental funds like the Global Environment Facility (GEF) and the International Fund for Agricultural Development (IFAD), as well as private sector investment, to expand land restoration projects."
      ],
      "body": "Tanzania's Minister of State, Vice President's Office (Union and Environment), Hamad Masauni, made the appeal at the 17th Conference of Parties to the United Nations Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD COP17) in Ulaanbaatar. The conference, themed “Restore Land, Restore Hope,” emphasizes increasing investment in land restoration, building resilience to drought, and sustainable management of grazing areas.\n\nMasauni highlighted that land degradation in Tanzania stems from deforestation, agricultural expansion, uncontrolled burning, soil erosion, unsustainable land use, and urban sprawl. He noted that Tanzania's participation in the conference offers opportunities to gain experience, technology, partnerships, and financial resources to improve sustainable management of grazing areas, restore degraded land, conserve forests and water sources, protect biodiversity, and enhance farmers' and pastoralists' resilience to environmental challenges.\n\nThimoth Mande, Tanzania's National Coordinator for the Convention, stated that international cooperation is crucial for Tanzania to address land degradation, biodiversity loss, and climate change comprehensively. He added that Tanzania's involvement aims to attract more resources from environmental funds like the Global Environment Facility (GEF) and the International Fund for Agricultural Development (IFAD), as well as private sector investment, to expand land restoration projects.",
