      "articleId": "6f1a7dc7f435269aea1c",
      "headline": "Libyan Attorney General uncovers 63,986 forged national ID numbers",
      "dek": "Investigations by the Attorney General's Office since 2017 have revealed widespread identity fraud, impacting government services and financial systems.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Libyan authorities have identified 63,986 forged national identification numbers that lack legitimate basis in the civil registry, according to Attorney General Al-Siddiq Al-Sour on August 17, 2022. An additional 88,819 national numbers were cited as incorrect, indicating further irregularities beyond proven forgeries. These findings are part of intensified investigations into broad identity fraud that began in 2017.",
        "The fraud has allowed individuals to obtain passports, public employment, government grants, and even voting rights. One case involved a foreign national who paid 10,000 dinars to a deceased man's father to use the stolen identity, leading to a marriage and four children whose legal status is now based on this crime. The Attorney General's office did not accuse the wife or children of collaboration.",
        "Further audits have uncovered financial implications, with the Social Solidarity Fund reporting 6,954 forged national IDs used to withdraw over three million dinars in pensions across five years, as announced on August 11, 2026. These financial reviews may overlap with other cases, so their numbers cannot be directly added to the official total of forged IDs."
      ],
      "body": "Libyan authorities have identified 63,986 forged national identification numbers that lack legitimate basis in the civil registry, according to Attorney General Al-Siddiq Al-Sour on August 17, 2022. An additional 88,819 national numbers were cited as incorrect, indicating further irregularities beyond proven forgeries. These findings are part of intensified investigations into broad identity fraud that began in 2017.\n\nThe fraud has allowed individuals to obtain passports, public employment, government grants, and even voting rights. One case involved a foreign national who paid 10,000 dinars to a deceased man's father to use the stolen identity, leading to a marriage and four children whose legal status is now based on this crime. The Attorney General's office did not accuse the wife or children of collaboration.\n\nFurther audits have uncovered financial implications, with the Social Solidarity Fund reporting 6,954 forged national IDs used to withdraw over three million dinars in pensions across five years, as announced on August 11, 2026. These financial reviews may overlap with other cases, so their numbers cannot be directly added to the official total of forged IDs.",
      "why": "Libyan citizens are affected by identity theft that can infiltrate families and create legal uncertainties for individuals, including children, whose status is built on fraudulent records.",
