      "articleId": "a60d32d738ac989165ef",
      "headline": "KoraLink empowers youth with health product sales in rural Rwanda",
      "dek": "A new initiative is creating income opportunities for young people, particularly women, by enabling them to sell essential health products in underserved communities.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "KoraLink is expanding access to essential health products in rural Rwanda by training and supporting young entrepreneurs. The program provides financially disadvantaged youth, aged 18 to 35, with product knowledge, business management skills, and an interest-free investment of Rwf360,000 in installments. This capital helps them establish businesses selling items like malaria prevention products, HIV and STI prevention products, sanitary pads, and water-treatment products.",
        "The initiative, a collaboration between the Mastercard Foundation, The Commons Project Foundation Africa (TCP Africa), Society for Family Health Rwanda (SFH Rwanda), and ICT Chamber, aims to overcome challenges of long travel distances and infrequent market days for rural residents. Dr. Richard Gakuba, Executive Secretary of TCP Africa, stated that the program supports youth, especially women and those in rural areas, to earn livelihoods.",
        "Agents like Bamurere Sylvie, 27, from Ngarama Sector, reported earning Rwf58,000 in profit within her first three months, with aspirations to become a livestock farmer. Niyokwizera Isaac, 26, from Rugarama Sector, grew his initial Rwf50,000 capital to order stock worth up to Rwf200,000 and invested in farmland and fertilizer for maize. Shakira Mwiza, 25, used profits to buy a computer and offer digital services, receiving working space from the Rwimiyaga Sector administration."
      ],
      "body": "KoraLink is expanding access to essential health products in rural Rwanda by training and supporting young entrepreneurs. The program provides financially disadvantaged youth, aged 18 to 35, with product knowledge, business management skills, and an interest-free investment of Rwf360,000 in installments. This capital helps them establish businesses selling items like malaria prevention products, HIV and STI prevention products, sanitary pads, and water-treatment products.\n\nThe initiative, a collaboration between the Mastercard Foundation, The Commons Project Foundation Africa (TCP Africa), Society for Family Health Rwanda (SFH Rwanda), and ICT Chamber, aims to overcome challenges of long travel distances and infrequent market days for rural residents. Dr. Richard Gakuba, Executive Secretary of TCP Africa, stated that the program supports youth, especially women and those in rural areas, to earn livelihoods.\n\nAgents like Bamurere Sylvie, 27, from Ngarama Sector, reported earning Rwf58,000 in profit within her first three months, with aspirations to become a livestock farmer. Niyokwizera Isaac, 26, from Rugarama Sector, grew his initial Rwf50,000 capital to order stock worth up to Rwf200,000 and invested in farmland and fertilizer for maize. Shakira Mwiza, 25, used profits to buy a computer and offer digital services, receiving working space from the Rwimiyaga Sector administration.",
